xpanding the right-hand side using the binomial theorem, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} e^{i(7-k)x} (-e^{-ix})^{7-k}.\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} e^{i(7-k)x} (-1)^{7-k} e^{-i(7-k)x}.\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} e^{i(7-2k)x}.\\]\n\nNow, we can use Euler's formula to express the complex exponentials in terms of sines and cosines:\n\\[e^{i\\theta} = \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta.\\]\n\nApplying this formula to the expression, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} (\\cos((7-2k)x) + i \\sin((7-2k)x)).\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\cos((7-2k)x) + \\frac{i}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin((7-2k)x).\\]\n\nNow, we can separate the real and imaginary parts of the expression:\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\cos((7-2k)x) + \\frac{i}{2^7 i^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin((7-2k)x).\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\cos((7-2k)x) + \\frac{i}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin((7-2k)x).\\]\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\cos(-\\theta) = \\cos \\theta$ and $\\sin(-\\theta) = -\\sin \\theta$ to simplify the expression:\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\cos((7-2k)x) - \\frac{i}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin((7-2k)x).\\]\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\cos \\theta = \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta)$ to rewrite the expression:\n\\[(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - (7-2k)x) - \\frac{i}{2^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^{7-k} \\sin((7-2k)x).\\]\n\nNow, w